WEBVTT

00:00:00.157 --> 00:00:02.187
Start by inspecting the source rows.

00:00:02.187 --> 00:00:05.417
Look for visible and invisible formatting differences.

00:00:05.417 --> 00:00:12.692
Notice missing emails or phones, blank values, inconsistent punctuation, and possible duplicate people.

00:00:12.692 --> 00:00:16.282
The purpose of this first query is not to fix anything.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:20.142
It is to build evidence about the source before defining rules.

00:00:20.142 --> 00:00:26.402
Connect this idea to a healthcare pipeline validating contact, demographic, and event fields.

00:00:26.402 --> 00:00:31.732
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:31.732 --> 00:00:38.862
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:38.862 --> 00:00:44.072
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:44.072 --> 00:00:49.360
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
